WEBVTT

00:31:27.337 --> 00:31:30.319
CA: Wow, well, coming from you,
that will surprise some people.

00:31:30.343 --> 00:31:33.052
DB: Well, believe me,
I never thought I would get there.

00:31:33.076 --> 00:31:35.155
CA: This is really
interesting to me, David.

00:31:35.179 --> 00:31:37.897
Tell me more about the evolution
of your thinking on this.

00:31:37.921 --> 00:31:39.202
DB: Well, a few years ago,

00:31:39.226 --> 00:31:41.910
Ta-Nehisi Coates wrote
a famous piece for "The Atlantic,"

00:31:41.934 --> 00:31:43.555
called "The Case For Reparations."

00:31:43.579 --> 00:31:45.834
And I read it learning
a lot about the redlining

00:31:45.858 --> 00:31:48.117
and some of the discriminatory
housing policies,

00:31:48.141 --> 00:31:51.619
but ultimately saying the idea of
reparations seems completely unworkable.

00:31:51.643 --> 00:31:54.763
Like, how do we find out who was
a descendant of an African slave,

00:31:54.787 --> 00:31:55.939
who was not?

00:31:55.963 --> 00:31:58.974
Are we supposed to give money
to Oprah Winfrey and LeBron James?

00:31:58.998 --> 00:32:00.573
Is it a matter of writing checks?